drive south\par
\cf2 You drive south.  You are in your red VW microbus which is parked in front of the church.  The road leads to the north from here.    You can only move east.\par
\cf1 \par
get out of bus\par
\cf2 You're on foot.  You are in front of a gothic wooden church.  The paint is peeling and the stained glass windows are in disrepair, but it retains much of its beauty and charm especially at this time of year.   Although the colors reached their peak a couple of weeks ago there are still enough orange leaves on the trees to remind you of a New England post card.  On the east side of the church you can make out Alice's garden.  Your VW microbus is parked here on a road heading north.  You'll have to get back in the bus if you want to go that way.  It's too far to walk.  There is a mailbox here.  You are no longer in the bus.\par
\cf1 \par
north\par
\cf2 You move north.\par
You are at a wooden gothic arched doorway.   You can move north, east, south, in and out.\par
\cf1 \par
north\par
\cf2 You move north.\par
The sanctuary has a high arched roof.  A few of the stained glass windows have been broken, but most are still intact.  They are mostly light blue and depict religious scenes, chalices and lillies.  Above the door is the impressive rose window which has images of Matthew, Mark, Luke and John.  At the northern end of the sanctuary is the area that once held the altar.  The attractiveness of the room is marred somewhat by the presence of a large pile of garbage in the corner.   To the west is the bell tower.   Alice and Facha are here.  You can move south, west and out.  Alice walks off to not moved.  "There you are," says Alice.  "I've been looking all over for you.  You got a phone call from Officer Obie.  He says he found an envelope with your name on it underneath a half a ton of garbage and he wants to know if you have any information about it."  You tell Alice you can not tell a lie, "I put that envelope under that garbage."  Alice takes you into the Sanctuary where she makes a phone call to Officer Obie.  Obie shows up a short time later and puts you in the back of the patrol car and takes you to the Police Officer Station.  You arrive at the Police Officer Station in handcuffs, which Obie removes.  He says, "Kid, I'm gonna put you in the cell.  You can't take all that stuff with you.  You're going to have to leave it here."  You leave the stuff behind Obie's desk.  For good measure, Obie takes your wallet and your belt.  You say, "Obie, I can understand you wanting my wallet, so I don't have any money to spend in the cell, but what do you want my belt for?"  He says, "Kid, we don't want any hangings."  You say, "Obie, did you think I was going to hang myself for littering?"  Obie is not as amused by this comment as future audiences might be.  He puts you in the cell and removes the toilet seat for good measure.  He closes the cell door behind you.  \par
\cf1 \par
wait\par
\cf2 Time passes...\par
You are in the cell.  Officer Obie walks towards you.  Officer Obie walks off to not moved.\par
\cf1 \par
call Alice\par
\cf2 You call Alice and ask her to bail you out of jail.  Alice has a few thoughts to share with you on the subject of dumping a half a ton of garbage and getting arrested while you're a guest at her house, but finally she arrives with bail money and you are released.    Alice walks towards you.  Officer Obie walks towards you.  Officer Obie says, "Well, you're free to go, kid.  Don't forget to show up in court.  And take whatever stuff you're going to need with you.  I don't want to see you back here again."\par
\cf1 \par
leave station\par
\cf2 You leave the station, prepared for your day in court.   The court room is in an old colonial building with uncomfortable wooden seats.   On the fateful day Obie shows up with 27 8X10 color glossy photos with circles and arrows and a paragraph of each one explaining what each one is to be used as evidence against you.  It doesn't matter.  Judge James E. Hannon is blind, and he is not going to look at the 27 8X10 color glossy pictures with circles and arrows and a paragraph on the back of each one explaining what each one is to be used as evidence against you.  And his seeing eye dog Susie is color blind.  Judge Hannon addresses you, "Son, how do you plead?"\par
\cf1 \par
guilty\par
\cf2 You are found guilty of the illegal disposal of rubbish and sentenced to a fine of $25 and ordered to pick up the garbage, which you do on the following day under an an icy rain.  You try to convince Officer Obie, who is overseeing the task, that you should only have to pick up the garbage that you actually put down there in the first place, but he's not buying it.  What a week you're having.  Two days later you have to report to Whitehall Street for your army physical....  This is the building where you get injected, inspected, detected, infected, neglected and selected.  You can only move north.  A Mean Looking Soldier asks you for your papers and looks you over as though he is inspecting any other piece of government property.  You believe you see a brief smirk on his face as he looks at your hair.  He hands you a piece of paper and says, "Kid, see the psychiatrist, room 604."\par
